<p>An enquiry for "guar gum" without a grade gets two very different answers depending on who reads it. A food technologist hears a thickener that must pass a food-additive specification. A frac-fluid engineer hears a polymer that must hydrate fast and crosslink on cue. Both start from the same Rajasthan seed, but the grades part ways at the mill, and the tests, the paperwork and the price follow them. This guide is for procurement and technical teams choosing between food and industrial guar gum from India. It sets out which parameters define each grade, which limits are written down in a public specification, how the two price differently, and where contracts most often go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p><strong>Reading the trade figures when you care about grade.</strong> HS 1302.32 takes in every grade of guar gum, and the heading also covers thickeners made from locust beans, so the table above can't tell you how much of India's trade was food grade. The yearly series is still useful. India's exports under the code rose from USD 339.4 million in 2021 to USD 508.1 million in 2022, then eased to USD 401.4 million in 2023 and USD 417.3 million in 2024. Volume moved less than value: about 240,637 MT in 2021, 266,165 MT in 2022, 211,412 MT in 2023 and 239,920 MT in 2024. The average value per kilogram therefore jumped by roughly a third between 2021 and 2022 and has stayed above the 2021 level since. That pattern is typical of a market where one demand stream, oilfield use, can swing sharply while food and technical demand stays steadier.</p>

<h2>From split to powder: where the two grades part company</h2>
<p>The guar seed has three parts: the husk, the germ and the endosperm. The endosperm holds the galactomannan, the long-chain polysaccharide that makes water thick. Processors crack the seed, strip out germ and husk, and sell the cleaned endosperm halves as <em>refined guar split</em>. The split is then hydrated, flaked, milled and screened into powder.</p>
<p>Up to that point the two grades can share a route. After it they diverge:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Food and pharma grade</strong> is milled from well-cleaned split under hygiene controls, and some processors wash the gum with ethanol or isopropanol to cut the microbial load. JECFA's specification allows this washing and caps the residual solvent at 1%. Every lot is tested against a food-additive specification before it is released.</li>
  <li><strong>Industrial grade</strong> is milled for performance. For hydraulic fracturing that usually means fast hydration and a predictable response to crosslinkers. For paper, textile printing, explosives and mining, it means a stable viscosity and good dispersion in the customer's process. Microbiology and heavy metals are not usually specified, because the product never enters food.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some industrial customers buy chemically modified guar (derivatives made by further reaction), which is a different product again. If your specification sheet names a derivative, send it with the enquiry, because plain guar gum powder will not match it.</p>

<h2>Viscosity: what the cP figure on a certificate really measures</h2>
<p>Viscosity is the number both grades are bought on, and it is only meaningful alongside its test conditions. A guar gum viscosity is normally read on a rotational viscometer from a dilute solution of stated strength, after a set hydration time at a set temperature. Change any of those and the figure changes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Concentration.</strong> Viscosity rises steeply with concentration, so a small difference in how the solution is made up gives a large difference in cP.</li>
  <li><strong>Hydration time.</strong> Guar keeps building viscosity after it disperses. A reading taken soon after mixing and one taken the next day on the same lot are not comparable.</li>
  <li><strong>Instrument, spindle and speed.</strong> Guar solutions thin under shear, so the reading depends on how fast the spindle turns.</li>
  <li><strong>Temperature and water quality.</strong> Warm water speeds hydration, and dissolved salts and pH shift the result.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our standard food-grade range runs from 3000 to 6000 cps, and industrial grades start at 5000 cps and go higher, each tested per batch. Oilfield buyers often care less about the final number than about how quickly viscosity builds in the first few minutes, because blending on a frac site happens on the move. Food buyers usually care about the final viscosity and its consistency from lot to lot, because that controls mouthfeel and stability in the finished product.</p>

<h2>Mesh size and hydration speed</h2>
<p>Mesh is the second grade parameter. Powder is sold at 80, 100, 200 or 300 mesh, where the higher number means finer particles. Finer powder offers more surface to water and hydrates faster, which is why oilfield and continuous-process users often choose 200 mesh or finer. Coarser powder disperses more easily without lumping, which suits batch mixing in food plants that don't use high-shear equipment.</p>
<p>Write mesh as a percentage passing a stated sieve (for example, a minimum share passing 200 mesh), not just as a name. Two lots called "200 mesh" can have quite different particle-size distributions, and the tail of coarse particles is what slows hydration.</p>

<h2>Purity and microbiology in the food-additive specification</h2>
<p>Food-grade guar gum is additive INS 412 (E 412 in the EU). The Joint FAO/WHO Expert Committee on Food Additives (JECFA) publishes its specification, and many national rules follow the same pattern. The JECFA monograph sets these limits:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">JECFA limit (guar gum, INS 412)</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Loss on drying</td><td>Not more than 15.0% (105°C, 5 h)</td><td>Moisture; our export lots are held tighter, at ≤12%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total ash</td><td>Not more than 1.5%</td><td>Mineral residue from soil and husk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acid-insoluble matter</td><td>Not more than 7.0%</td><td>Husk and other insoluble residue left from milling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protein</td><td>Not more than 10.0% (N × 6.25)</td><td>Germ carried into the endosperm; high protein means poorer separation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Borate</td><td>Absent</td><td>Rules out crosslinked material in a food lot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Residual ethanol / isopropanol</td><td>Not more than 1%</td><td>Applies to solvent-washed gum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lead</td><td>Not more than 2 mg/kg</td><td>Heavy-metal contamination</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total plate count</td><td>Not more than 5,000 CFU/g</td><td>General hygiene of milling and packing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yeasts and moulds</td><td>Not more than 500 CFU/g</td><td>Storage and moisture history</td></tr>
<tr><td>E. coli / Salmonella</td><td>Negative in 1g / negative in 25g</td><td>Pathogen control</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>National rules add their own layer. In the United States, 21 CFR 184.1339 lists guar gum as GRAS, requires it to meet the Food Chemicals Codex specification and sets maximum use levels by food category, from 0.35% in baked goods to 2.0% in fats and oils and processed vegetables, with 0.5% for categories not listed. In the EU, food-grade guar gum from India also travels with a PCP (pentachlorophenol) analytical report endorsed by SHEFEXIL. Your market may add more, so send your specification sheet and we will test to it.</p>

<h2>What drilling and technical buyers specify instead</h2>
<p>An industrial specification reads like a performance test rather than a purity list. For fracturing grades the usual items are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hydration rate</strong>: viscosity at a few set times after mixing, often within minutes, to show how quickly the gel builds.</li>
  <li><strong>Final or base-gel viscosity</strong>: under the buyer's own concentration, temperature and instrument conditions.</li>
  <li><strong>Crosslink response</strong>: guar gels with borate, which is the basis of many frac fluids. JECFA uses the same reaction as an identity test, and it is why borate must be absent from a food lot.</li>
  <li><strong>Insoluble residue</strong>: residue left after the gel breaks can reduce flow in the formation, so drilling buyers watch it closely.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture, pH and particle size</strong>: these control dosing and dispersion in field equipment.</li>
</ul>
<p>Paper, textile-printing and mining buyers each have their own short list, usually viscosity at their own concentration plus mesh and moisture. None of them normally asks for a microbiology panel. If yours does, say so, because it changes how the lot is milled and tested.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The certificate of analysis is the document that separates the two grades on paper, so make it match your contract line for line. For food grade that means the viscosity method as well as the result, mesh as percentage passing, moisture, protein, ash, acid-insoluble residue and the microbiology panel. For industrial grade it means the hydration and viscosity readings under your test conditions. Guar gum is Free for export under DGFT's ITC(HS) schedule, and we confirm the current policy position for HS 1302.32 when each order is placed. The grade and end use should appear on the commercial invoice, so the documents and the goods can be checked against each other at the destination.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>Why food and industrial offers move differently</h2>
<p>Both grades are priced off the same raw material, guar seed, so the seed market sets the floor for everything. Guar is a monsoon crop grown mainly in Rajasthan and harvested September to November, and rainfall in the growing belt decides how much seed reaches the mills each year. A weak crop lifts every grade at once.</p>
<p>Above that floor the grades behave differently. Industrial demand, and above all oilfield demand, is the swing factor. When drilling and fracturing activity picks up, mills see a surge of orders for fast-hydrating powder, seed stocks are drawn down, and prices can move quickly. The 2021-2022 jump in India's average export value per kilogram shows how sharp that can be. Food-grade demand moves more slowly, but food-grade buyers still feel oilfield cycles, because they draw on the same seed.</p>
<p>Within each grade the specification adds cost in steps. Tighter viscosity bands, finer mesh, lower protein and a full microbiology panel all mean more processing, more testing or lower yield. Solvent washing and the EU PCP report add further cost where they apply. Exchange rates and freight to your port complete the picture.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your grade, viscosity range and method, mesh, test panel, packaging and destination port. The more of these you send, the faster and closer the first quote will be.</p>
